'<p class="l-text"><strong>Data cleaning</strong> is the unglamorous but essential skill that separates beginners from professionals. In real-world projects, 60\u201380% of your time will be spent cleaning, reshaping, and validating data before any analysis or modeling. Messy data contains missing values, duplicates, wrong types, inconsistent strings, and outliers \u2014 and Pandas gives you a powerful toolkit to handle all of them systematically.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The data cleaning paradox:</strong> The cleaner your data, the simpler your models need to be. Teams that invest heavily in data cleaning often outperform teams with sophisticated models but messy data. In NLP, cleaning text data properly can improve model accuracy by 5\u201315% before you even change the model.</div>'
+ '<div class="lesson-outcomes" style="background:rgba(200,169,110,0.06);border-left:3px solid #c8a96e;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.08em;color:#c8a96e;margin-bottom:0.6rem">📍 WHAT YOU\'LL LEARN</div><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;line-height:1.65;font-size:0.93rem;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.92)"><li>Detect missing values with .isna() and quantify them per column</li><li>Drop or fill NaNs with .dropna(), .fillna() and forward/backward fill</li><li>Identify and remove duplicate rows with .duplicated() and .drop_duplicates()</li><li>Detect outliers with IQR and z-score and decide to clip, drop or keep them</li><li>Standardise text columns by stripping, lower-casing and fixing typos</li><li>Convert dtypes with .astype() and validate with .info() and .describe()</li></ul></div>'


/* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: Missing Data
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Missing Data</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Missing data (represented as <code>NaN</code>, <code>None</code>, or <code>NaT</code> for timestamps) is the most common data quality issue. Before handling it, you need to <strong>detect</strong> where it exists, <strong>understand</strong> why it is missing, and then decide whether to <strong>drop</strong>, <strong>fill</strong>, or <strong>interpolate</strong> it.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Detect</div><div class="card-body">isna() / isnull() return True for missing values. notna() / notnull() return True for valid values. Use .sum() to count nulls per column.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Drop</div><div class="card-body">dropna() removes rows or columns with missing values. Use how, thresh, and subset parameters for fine control.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Fill</div><div class="card-body">fillna() replaces NaN with a value, forward-fill (ffill), or back-fill (bfill). Different columns may need different strategies.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Interpolate</div><div class="card-body">interpolate() estimates missing values using linear, polynomial, or time-based interpolation. Perfect for time series data.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Create a DataFrame with missing values</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>: [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="kw">None</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>, <span class="str">"Frank"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"age"</span>: [<span class="num">25</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">28</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">40</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>: [<span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, np.nan, np.nan, <span class="num">91</span>, <span class="num">85</span>],\n    <span class="str">"city"</span>: [<span class="str">"NYC"</span>, <span class="str">"LA"</span>, <span class="str">"NYC"</span>, np.nan, np.nan, <span class="str">"SF"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- DETECTION ----</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">isna</span>())            <span class="cm"># Boolean DataFrame of missing values</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())      <span class="cm"># Count nulls per column</span>\n<span class="cm"># name     1</span>\n<span class="cm"># age      2</span>\n<span class="cm"># score    2</span>\n<span class="cm"># city     2</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())  <span class="cm"># Total nulls: 7</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Percentage of missing per column</span>\nmissing_pct = df.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">mean</span>() * <span class="num">100</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(missing_pct.<span class="fn">round</span>(<span class="num">1</span>))\n<span class="cm"># name     16.7%</span>\n<span class="cm"># age      33.3%</span>\n<span class="cm"># score    33.3%</span>\n<span class="cm"># city     33.3%</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Rows with ANY missing value</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[df.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">any</span>(axis=<span class="num">1</span>)])\n\n<span class="cm"># Rows with ALL values present</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[df.<span class="fn">notna</span>().<span class="fn">all</span>(axis=<span class="num">1</span>)])</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># ---- DROPPING ----</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop rows where ANY value is NaN (default)</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">dropna</span>()                    <span class="cm"># only Alice and Frank survive</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop rows where ALL values are NaN</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">dropna</span>(how=<span class="str">"all"</span>)            <span class="cm"># only drops completely empty rows</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop rows with fewer than 3 non-null values</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">dropna</span>(thresh=<span class="num">3</span>)             <span class="cm"># keep rows with at least 3 valid values</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop rows where specific columns are NaN</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">dropna</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"name"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>])  <span class="cm"># only check these columns</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop columns instead of rows</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">dropna</span>(axis=<span class="num">1</span>)               <span class="cm"># drop columns with ANY NaN</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">dropna</span>(axis=<span class="num">1</span>, thresh=<span class="num">5</span>)    <span class="cm"># keep columns with at least 5 valid values</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># ---- FILLING ----</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Fill with a constant value</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(<span class="num">0</span>)           <span class="cm"># replace NaN with 0</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"name"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(<span class="str">"Unknown"</span>)   <span class="cm"># replace NaN with string</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Fill with column mean/median/mode</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(df[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>())      <span class="cm"># mean imputation</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(df[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">median</span>())    <span class="cm"># median (robust to outliers)</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"city"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(df[<span class="str">"city"</span>].<span class="fn">mode</span>()[<span class="num">0</span>]) <span class="cm"># most common value</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Forward fill and backward fill</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">ffill</span>()   <span class="cm"># propagate last valid value forward</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">bfill</span>()   <span class="cm"># propagate next valid value backward</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Fill different columns with different values</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">fillna</span>({<span class="str">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"Unknown"</span>, <span class="str">"age"</span>: df[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">median</span>(), <span class="str">"score"</span>: <span class="num">0</span>})\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- INTERPOLATION ----</span>\nts = pd.<span class="fn">Series</span>([<span class="num">1</span>, np.nan, np.nan, <span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">7</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(ts.<span class="fn">interpolate</span>())          <span class="cm"># linear: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(ts.<span class="fn">interpolate</span>(method=<span class="str">"polynomial"</span>, order=<span class="num">2</span>))  <span class="cm"># quadratic fit</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">WHEN TO DROP</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Missing completely at random (MCAR)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Less than 5% of data is missing</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Large dataset where losing rows is acceptable</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">The missing pattern carries no information</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">WHEN TO FILL / INTERPOLATE</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Missing not at random (MNAR) \u2014 pattern is informative</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Small dataset where every row matters</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Time series with gaps between measurements</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Feature is critical for downstream model</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><strong>Think about it:</strong> Mean imputation is easy, but it reduces variance and weakens correlations between features. If 30% of your "age" column is missing and you fill it with the mean, you are telling your model that 30% of people are exactly average age. For ML pipelines, consider using <code>sklearn.impute.KNNImputer</code> or <code>IterativeImputer</code> instead.</div>'

