/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: Duplicates
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Duplicates</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Duplicate rows silently corrupt your analysis. They inflate counts, skew averages, and cause data leakage in ML train/test splits. Pandas provides <code>duplicated()</code> to detect and <code>drop_duplicates()</code> to remove them, with fine-grained control over which columns to check and which occurrence to keep.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"text"</span>: [<span class="str">"hello world"</span>, <span class="str">"foo bar"</span>, <span class="str">"hello world"</span>, <span class="str">"baz qux"</span>, <span class="str">"foo bar"</span>, <span class="str">"new text"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"label"</span>: [<span class="str">"pos"</span>, <span class="str">"neg"</span>, <span class="str">"pos"</span>, <span class="str">"pos"</span>, <span class="str">"neg"</span>, <span class="str">"pos"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"source"</span>: [<span class="str">"web"</span>, <span class="str">"api"</span>, <span class="str">"web"</span>, <span class="str">"manual"</span>, <span class="str">"web"</span>, <span class="str">"api"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- DETECTION ----</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>())          <span class="cm"># Boolean: True for duplicate rows</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0    False</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1    False</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2     True   \u2190 duplicate of row 0</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3    False</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4     True   \u2190 duplicate of row 1</span>\n<span class="cm"># 5    False</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())    <span class="cm"># 2 duplicate rows</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Check duplicates on specific columns only</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"text"</span>])  <span class="cm"># only check \'text\' column</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Keep last instead of first</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>(keep=<span class="str">"last"</span>)      <span class="cm"># marks FIRST occurrence as duplicate</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>(keep=<span class="kw">False</span>)       <span class="cm"># marks ALL occurrences as duplicate</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># View all duplicated rows (including originals)</span>\ndupes = df[df.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"text"</span>], keep=<span class="kw">False</span>)]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(dupes)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># ---- REMOVAL ----</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop exact duplicate rows (keep first occurrence)</span>\ndf_clean = df.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>()\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">len</span>(df), <span class="str">"\u2192"</span>, <span class="fn">len</span>(df_clean))  <span class="cm"># 6 \u2192 4</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop based on specific columns</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"text"</span>])           <span class="cm"># unique texts only</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"text"</span>, <span class="str">"label"</span>])  <span class="cm"># unique (text, label) pairs</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Keep last occurrence</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"text"</span>], keep=<span class="str">"last"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Drop ALL occurrences of duplicated rows</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"text"</span>], keep=<span class="kw">False</span>)  <span class="cm"># removes both copies</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- NLP PRACTICAL: Deduplicating a text corpus ----</span>\ncorpus = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"text"</span>: [\n        <span class="str">"The model performs well on NLP tasks."</span>,\n        <span class="str">"the model performs well on nlp tasks."</span>,  <span class="cm"># case variation</span>\n        <span class="str">"The model  performs well on NLP tasks."</span>, <span class="cm"># extra space</span>\n        <span class="str">"A different sentence entirely."</span>\n    ]\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Normalize before dedup: lowercase + collapse whitespace</span>\ncorpus[<span class="str">"normalized"</span>] = (corpus[<span class="str">"text"</span>]\n    .str.<span class="fn">lower</span>()\n    .str.<span class="fn">strip</span>()\n    .str.<span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="str">r"\\s+"</span>, <span class="str">" "</span>, regex=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n)\ncorpus_clean = corpus.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"normalized"</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">len</span>(corpus), <span class="str">"\u2192"</span>, <span class="fn">len</span>(corpus_clean))  <span class="cm"># 4 \u2192 2</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>NLP warning:</strong> In text datasets, duplicates are rarely exact. Normalize text (lowercase, strip whitespace, remove punctuation) before checking for duplicates. Also watch for <strong>near-duplicates</strong> \u2014 sentences that differ by one or two words. Libraries like <code>datasketch</code> (MinHash LSH) can detect near-duplicates at scale.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: Type Conversion
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Type Conversion</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Pandas often infers column types incorrectly when reading data: numbers stored as strings, dates as objects, categories as full strings wasting memory. Fixing types is essential \u2014 wrong types cause silent bugs (e.g., sorting "10" before "2" as strings) and waste memory (storing "yes"/"no" as 64-byte object instead of 1-byte bool).</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">astype()</div><div class="card-body">General type casting. Works for int, float, str, bool, category. Raises errors on invalid values by default.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">to_numeric()</div><div class="card-body">Smart numeric conversion. errors=\'coerce\' turns unparseable values into NaN instead of crashing. Essential for dirty data.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">to_datetime()</div><div class="card-body">Parse dates from strings. Handles many formats automatically. Use format= for specific patterns (faster and more reliable).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Categorical</div><div class="card-body">pd.Categorical or astype(\'category\'). Reduces memory dramatically for low-cardinality columns (e.g., gender, country).</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"price"</span>: [<span class="str">"10.5"</span>, <span class="str">"20"</span>, <span class="str">"N/A"</span>, <span class="str">"15.3"</span>, <span class="str">"bad"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"date"</span>: [<span class="str">"2024-01-15"</span>, <span class="str">"2024-02-20"</span>, <span class="str">"2024/03/10"</span>, <span class="str">"March 5, 2024"</span>, <span class="str">"2024-04-01"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"is_valid"</span>: [<span class="str">"yes"</span>, <span class="str">"no"</span>, <span class="str">"yes"</span>, <span class="str">"yes"</span>, <span class="str">"no"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"category"</span>: [<span class="str">"A"</span>, <span class="str">"B"</span>, <span class="str">"A"</span>, <span class="str">"C"</span>, <span class="str">"B"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.dtypes)\n<span class="cm"># price       object  \u2190 should be float</span>\n<span class="cm"># date        object  \u2190 should be datetime</span>\n<span class="cm"># is_valid    object  \u2190 should be bool</span>\n<span class="cm"># category    object  \u2190 should be category</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- to_numeric: handles errors gracefully ----</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"price"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[<span class="str">"price"</span>], errors=<span class="str">"coerce"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[<span class="str">"price"</span>])\n<span class="cm"># 0    10.5</span>\n<span class="cm"># 1    20.0</span>\n<span class="cm"># 2     NaN   \u2190 "N/A" became NaN</span>\n<span class="cm"># 3    15.3</span>\n<span class="cm"># 4     NaN   \u2190 "bad" became NaN</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- to_datetime: auto-detects formats ----</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"date"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_datetime</span>(df[<span class="str">"date"</span>], format=<span class="str">"mixed"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df[<span class="str">"date"</span>].dtype)  <span class="cm"># datetime64[ns]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Specific format (much faster for large data)</span>\n<span class="cm"># pd.to_datetime(df["date"], format="%Y-%m-%d")</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Boolean mapping ----</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"is_valid"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"is_valid"</span>].<span class="fn">map</span>({<span class="str">"yes"</span>: <span class="kw">True</span>, <span class="str">"no"</span>: <span class="kw">False</span>})\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Category type (saves memory) ----</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"category"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"category"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"category"</span>)\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.dtypes)\n<span class="cm"># price       float64</span>\n<span class="cm"># date        datetime64[ns]</span>\n<span class="cm"># is_valid    bool</span>\n<span class="cm"># category    category</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># ---- MEMORY OPTIMIZATION ----</span>\n<span class="cm"># Real-world impact of choosing the right types</span>\n\n<span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\nn = <span class="num">100_000</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"id"</span>: np.<span class="fn">arange</span>(n),\n    <span class="str">"status"</span>: np.random.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="str">"active"</span>, <span class="str">"inactive"</span>, <span class="str">"pending"</span>], n),\n    <span class="str">"value"</span>: np.random.<span class="fn">rand</span>(n) * <span class="num">100</span>,\n    <span class="str">"flag"</span>: np.random.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>], n)\n})\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Before:"</span>, df.memory_usage(deep=<span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>() / <span class="num">1e6</span>, <span class="str">"MB"</span>)\n<span class="cm"># Before: ~6.5 MB</span>\n\ndf[<span class="str">"id"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"id"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"int32"</span>)           <span class="cm"># 8 bytes \u2192 4 bytes</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"status"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"status"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"category"</span>)  <span class="cm"># ~60 bytes \u2192 1 byte</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"value"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"value"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"float32"</span>)    <span class="cm"># 8 bytes \u2192 4 bytes</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"flag"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"flag"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"int8"</span>)         <span class="cm"># 8 bytes \u2192 1 byte</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"After:"</span>, df.memory_usage(deep=<span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>() / <span class="num">1e6</span>, <span class="str">"MB"</span>)\n<span class="cm"># After: ~1.1 MB  (83% reduction!)</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Check dtypes first</div><div class="step-detail">Run <code>df.dtypes</code> and <code>df.info()</code> immediately after loading data. Any column showing <code>object</code> when it should be numeric or datetime is a red flag.</div></div></div>'
