+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> The dramatic impact of systematic string cleaning. Before cleaning, the dataset had 136 string issues across categories. After applying strip, lower, regex replace, and format standardization, only 5 edge cases remain.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: Outlier Detection
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">5. Outlier Detection</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Outliers are data points that deviate significantly from the rest of the distribution. They can be <strong>legitimate</strong> (a billionaire in income data) or <strong>errors</strong> (a negative age). Blindly removing outliers destroys information; blindly keeping them distorts your model. You need systematic methods to detect them and domain knowledge to decide what to do.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Z-score outlier rule — how many standard deviations a value lies from the mean:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$z = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}, \\quad \\text{flag if } |z| > 3$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">z</div><div class="card-body">Z-score — standardized distance from the mean in units of standard deviation.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x</div><div class="card-body">The raw observation we are testing for outlier status.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">μ, σ</div><div class="card-body">Mean and standard deviation of the column. Both are sensitive to outliers themselves — a single huge value inflates σ and hides other anomalies.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">|z| > 3</div><div class="card-body">Standard threshold. Under a normal distribution only 0.27% of points fall beyond ±3σ — anything farther is suspicious.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">When to use</div><div class="card-body">Best for roughly normal, large datasets. Avoid on heavily skewed data — use IQR instead.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>IQR rule — robust outlier detection that does not assume normality:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\text{outlier if } x < Q_1 - 1.5\\,\\text{IQR} \\;\\;\\text{or}\\;\\; x > Q_3 + 1.5\\,\\text{IQR}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Q1, Q3</div><div class="card-body">First and third quartiles — 25th and 75th percentiles of the data. Together they cover the middle 50%.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">IQR</div><div class="card-body">Interquartile range = Q3 − Q1. Robust spread measure: extreme values cannot inflate it the way they inflate σ.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">1.5 multiplier</div><div class="card-body">Tukey\'s convention. For more aggressive flagging use 1.5; for "extreme" outliers use 3.0.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Boxplot whiskers</div><div class="card-body">Standard boxplot whiskers exactly span [Q1 − 1.5·IQR, Q3 + 1.5·IQR]. Anything beyond is plotted as a dot — visual outlier detection.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Min-max normalization — rescale a column to [0, 1]:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$x\' = \\frac{x - x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max} - x_{\\min}}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x\'</div><div class="card-body">Normalized value, always in [0, 1]. The minimum maps to 0; the maximum maps to 1.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x_min, x_max</div><div class="card-body">Smallest and largest values in the column. Both are highly sensitive to outliers — one extreme value compresses everything else into a tiny range.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">vs Z-score</div><div class="card-body">Min-max preserves the distribution shape but is fragile to outliers. Z-score (standardization) is more robust and the usual default in ML.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Use cases</div><div class="card-body">Image pixel scaling, neural networks expecting bounded inputs, models that compare features on a [0, 1] scale.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">IQR METHOD (Robust)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Q1 = 25th percentile, Q3 = 75th percentile</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">IQR = Q3 - Q1</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Outlier if x < Q1 - 1.5*IQR or x > Q3 + 1.5*IQR</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Works well for skewed distributions</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">Z-SCORE METHOD (Parametric)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">z = (x - mean) / std</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Outlier if |z| > 3 (or 2.5, 2)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Assumes approximately normal distribution</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Sensitive to extreme values in mean/std</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\nnp.random.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"salary"</span>: np.<span class="fn">concatenate</span>([\n        np.random.<span class="fn">normal</span>(<span class="num">50000</span>, <span class="num">10000</span>, <span class="num">97</span>),  <span class="cm"># normal salaries</span>\n        [<span class="num">200000</span>, <span class="num">350000</span>, <span class="num">5000</span>]               <span class="cm"># outliers</span>\n    ])\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- IQR METHOD ----</span>\nQ1 = df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">quantile</span>(<span class="num">0.25</span>)\nQ3 = df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">quantile</span>(<span class="num">0.75</span>)\nIQR = Q3 - Q1\nlower = Q1 - <span class="num">1.5</span> * IQR\nupper = Q3 + <span class="num">1.5</span> * IQR\n\noutliers_iqr = df[(df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>] < lower) | (df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>] > upper)]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"IQR bounds: [{lower:.0f}, {upper:.0f}]"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Outliers found: {len(outliers_iqr)}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Z-SCORE METHOD ----</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"z_score"</span>] = (df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>] - df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>()) / df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">std</span>()\noutliers_z = df[df[<span class="str">"z_score"</span>].<span class="fn">abs</span>() > <span class="num">3</span>]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Z-score outliers: {len(outliers_z)}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- CLIPPING (Winsorization) ----</span>\n<span class="cm"># Cap extreme values instead of removing them</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"salary_clipped"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">clip</span>(lower=lower, upper=upper)\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- QUANTILE-BASED CLIPPING ----</span>\n<span class="cm"># Cap at 1st and 99th percentiles</span>\np01 = df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">quantile</span>(<span class="num">0.01</span>)\np99 = df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">quantile</span>(<span class="num">0.99</span>)\ndf[<span class="str">"salary_winsorized"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"salary"</span>].<span class="fn">clip</span>(lower=p01, upper=p99)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Remove when outlier is clearly an error</div><div class="step-detail">Negative ages, impossible temperatures, or data entry mistakes like typing 10000 instead of 100. These are not real data points.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Clip when outlier is real but extreme</div><div class="step-detail">A $350K salary is real, but letting it dominate a model trained on $50K averages is harmful. Clipping preserves the direction (high) while limiting the magnitude.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Keep when outlier is the signal</div><div class="step-detail">Fraud detection, anomaly detection, rare disease identification \u2014 the outliers ARE what you are looking for. Removing them defeats the purpose.</div></div></div>'
+ '</div>'

/* --- Plotly: Box Plot Outliers (EN) --- */
+ '<div id="plot-outlier-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ 'var np_seed=42;'
+ 'var normal=[];for(var i=0;i<97;i++){var u=0,v=0,s=0;do{u=Math.random()*2-1;v=Math.random()*2-1;s=u*u+v*v}while(s>=1||s===0);var mul=Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(s)/s);normal.push(50000+10000*u*mul)}'
+ 'normal.push(200000,350000,5000);'
+ 'var q1=normal.slice().sort(function(a,b){return a-b})[Math.floor(normal.length*0.25)];'
+ 'var q3=normal.slice().sort(function(a,b){return a-b})[Math.floor(normal.length*0.75)];'
+ 'var iqr=q3-q1;var lo=q1-1.5*iqr;var hi=q3+1.5*iqr;'
+ 'var clipped=normal.map(function(v){return Math.max(lo,Math.min(hi,v))});'
+ 'var t1={y:normal,type:"box",name:"Original",marker:{color:"#f87171"},boxpoints:"outliers",jitter:0.3,pointpos:-1.5};'
+ 'var t2={y:clipped,type:"box",name:"After Clipping",marker:{color:"#4de87a"},boxpoints:"outliers",jitter:0.3,pointpos:-1.5};'
+ 'var layout={title:{text:"Salary Distribution: Outlier Detection (IQR Method)",font:{color:"#ebe6dc",size:14}},paper_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",plot_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",font:{color:"#ebe6dc"},yaxis:{title:"Salary ($)",gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.06)"},margin:{t:50,r:30,b:40,l:70},legend:{font:{color:"#ebe6dc"}},showlegend:true};'
+ 'Plotly.newPlot("plot-outlier-en",[t1,t2],layout,{responsive:true,displayModeBar:false});'
+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> Box plots comparing original data (with outliers at $5K, $200K, $350K) versus clipped data. The IQR method identifies points beyond the whiskers as outliers. After clipping, the distribution is bounded without losing the data points entirely.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 6: Complete Cleaning Pipeline
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">6. Complete Cleaning Pipeline</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">In practice, you never apply just one cleaning step \u2014 you chain them into a <strong>pipeline</strong>. The order matters: fix types first (so numeric operations work), then handle nulls (which type conversion may have created), then remove duplicates, clean strings, and finally handle outliers. Here is a complete real-world example.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Fix Data Types</div><div class="step-detail">Convert strings to numbers, parse dates, map booleans. Use <code>errors=\'coerce\'</code> to avoid crashes. This step often creates new NaN values from unparseable entries.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Handle Missing Values</div><div class="step-detail">Assess the missingness pattern (MCAR, MAR, MNAR). Drop columns with >50% missing. Fill numeric with median, categorical with mode. Interpolate time series.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Remove Duplicates</div><div class="step-detail">Normalize text columns first (lowercase, strip whitespace), then deduplicate on the key columns. Log how many rows were removed.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Clean Strings</div><div class="step-detail">Strip whitespace, normalize case, remove special characters, standardize formats (phone numbers, emails). Use regex for complex patterns.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Handle Outliers</div><div class="step-detail">Apply IQR or z-score detection. Clip extreme values for numeric features. Document decisions on which outliers to keep vs remove.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">6</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Validate</div><div class="step-detail">Re-check dtypes, null counts, duplicate counts, and value ranges. Assert constraints: no negative ages, valid email formats, scores between 0\u2013100.</div></div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># STEP 0: Load messy data</span>\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\nraw = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>:     [<span class="str">"  Alice "</span>, <span class="str">"BOB"</span>, <span class="str">" carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"eve"</span>, <span class="str">"BOB"</span>, <span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="kw">None</span>],\n    <span class="str">"age"</span>:      [<span class="str">"25"</span>, <span class="str">"30"</span>, <span class="str">"twenty"</span>, <span class="str">"35"</span>, <span class="str">"22"</span>, <span class="str">"30"</span>, <span class="str">"25"</span>, <span class="str">"40"</span>],\n    <span class="str">"score"</span>:    [<span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, np.nan, <span class="num">160</span>, <span class="num">91</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, <span class="num">88</span>, np.nan],\n    <span class="str">"email"</span>:    [<span class="str">"Alice@Test.COM"</span>, <span class="str">"bob@test.com"</span>, <span class="str">"carol@test.com"</span>,\n                 <span class="str">"N/A"</span>, <span class="str">"eve@test.com"</span>, <span class="str">"bob@test.com"</span>, <span class="str">"alice@test.com"</span>, np.nan],\n    <span class="str">"joined"</span>:   [<span class="str">"2024-01-15"</span>, <span class="str">"2024-02-20"</span>, <span class="str">"2024/03/10"</span>,\n                 <span class="str">"March 2024"</span>, <span class="str">"2024-04-01"</span>, <span class="str">"2024-02-20"</span>, <span class="str">"2024-01-15"</span>, <span class="str">"2024-05-01"</span>]\n})\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"=== RAW DATA ==="</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Shape: {raw.shape}"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Nulls:\\n{raw.isna().sum()}"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Dtypes:\\n{raw.dtypes}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># STEP 1: Fix types</span>\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\ndf = raw.<span class="fn">copy</span>()\ndf[<span class="str">"age"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[<span class="str">"age"</span>], errors=<span class="str">"coerce"</span>)\ndf[<span class="str">"joined"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_datetime</span>(df[<span class="str">"joined"</span>], errors=<span class="str">"coerce"</span>, format=<span class="str">"mixed"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"\\nAfter type fix \u2014 new NaNs in age: {df[\'age\'].isna().sum()}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># STEP 2: Handle missing values</span>\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"name"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(<span class="str">"Unknown"</span>, inplace=<span class="kw">True</span>)\ndf[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(df[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">median</span>(), inplace=<span class="kw">True</span>)\ndf[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(df[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">median</span>(), inplace=<span class="kw">True</span>)\ndf[<span class="str">"email"</span>].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(<span class="str">"missing@na.com"</span>, inplace=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"After null handling \u2014 total NaN: {df.isna().sum().sum()}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># STEP 3: Clean strings (before dedup!)</span>\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"name"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"name"</span>].str.<span class="fn">strip</span>().str.<span class="fn">title</span>()\ndf[<span class="str">"email"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"email"</span>].str.<span class="fn">strip</span>().str.<span class="fn">lower</span>()\ndf[<span class="str">"email"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"email"</span>].<span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="str">"n/a"</span>, <span class="str">"missing@na.com"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># STEP 4: Remove duplicates</span>\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\nbefore_dedup = <span class="fn">len</span>(df)\ndf = df.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"name"</span>, <span class="str">"email"</span>], keep=<span class="str">"first"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Dedup: {before_dedup} \u2192 {len(df)} rows"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># STEP 5: Handle outliers</span>\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># Score must be 0\u2013100 (domain constraint)</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"score"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">clip</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">100</span>)\n<span class="cm"># Age must be positive and reasonable</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"age"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">clip</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">120</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="cm"># STEP 6: Validate</span>\n<span class="cm"># ============================================================</span>\n<span class="kw">assert</span> df[<span class="str">"age"</span>].<span class="fn">between</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">120</span>).<span class="fn">all</span>(), <span class="str">"Age out of range!"</span>\n<span class="kw">assert</span> df[<span class="str">"score"</span>].<span class="fn">between</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">100</span>).<span class="fn">all</span>(), <span class="str">"Score out of range!"</span>\n<span class="kw">assert</span> df.<span class="fn">isna</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>() <= <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="str">"Too many remaining nulls!"</span>\n<span class="kw">assert</span> df.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>(subset=[<span class="str">"name"</span>,<span class="str">"email"</span>]).<span class="fn">sum</span>() == <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="str">"Duplicates remain!"</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"\\n=== CLEAN DATA ==="</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"\\nShape: {df.shape}"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Dtypes:\\n{df.dtypes}"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Data Quality Score (EN) --- */
